  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1253 PM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nClouds are clearing out from west to east this afternoon as a\ncold/occluded front passes through. While the air is moist and \nshould become unstable this afternoon ahead of the front, the \nclouds ongoing right now are likely to inhibit convective \ninitiation in our area. Models have really continued to trend \ndry this afternoon with thunderstorms possible into the UP and \nnortheast Wisconsin. We will have to keep a keen eye on things \nthis afternoon in case there does happen to be a bit more \nclearing than expected and storms can initiate further west, but\nright now confidence is high that northwest Wisconsin will \nremain dry this afternoon.\n\nInto tonight, nearly vertically stacked low pressure will \nslowly drift east up in Canada and we will be within the warm\nsector to the south with quiet (but warm) weather expected\ntonight into Tuesday. Light winds tonight may result in some\nfog tonight, but widespread dense fog is not likely. Tuesday\nafternoon, dry and breezy conditions could result in some\nelevated fire weather, mainly for northwest Wisconsin. \n\nA quasi-stationary front may develop and produce an area of\nshowers/storms Tuesday night. Models differ on how far north the\nshowers/storms may make it, but east-central Minnesota and\nnorthwest Wisconsin have the best chances for rain Tuesday night\ninto Wednesday morning. Not a lot of instability is expected, so\nsevere storms are not expected either.\n\nWednesday is looking like an \"in-between\" kind of day with low\npressure well to our northeast and ridging off to our west.\nWinds will likely be light from approximately the northwest,\ntemperatures will be warm with highs around 80, and clouds may\ndecrease through the day with potentially some drier air moving\nin.\n\nOn Thursday, we may end up in a \"dirty ridge\" pattern with warm\nair and moisture advection from the south and potential for a\nwarm front to bring some rain/storm chances in. This sort of\nunorganized, slightly chaotic pattern underneath a broad ridge\nlooks to continue into the weekend, so look for more occasional\nshower/storm chances and warm weather. While there are no clear\nsignals for severe weather at this time, it's possible that\ningredients could aligns for a few severe storms on some of the\ndays from Thursday into next weekend. Signals for widespread,\ndrought-improving rainfall don't appear imminent, but certainly\nsome places could pick up some decent rain if thunderstorms\ndevelop.",
